When I first learned OOP in Java, I could repeat the four pillars like a poem: encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction. But if you had asked me what actually happens in memory when I write new Dog(), I would have gone quiet.
That gap is what confused me most. I knew the words, but not the machine behind them. And without that picture, things like "why did changing one object change another?" or "how does Java know which method to call?" felt like magic.
In this post, we'll learn each OOP idea and then peek behind the curtain to see what the JVM does. If you've read my post on JDK, JRE and JVM, this is a nice follow-up, because the JVM is the star here too.
First, a quick picture of memory
Before any OOP, you need a simple map of where things live. The JVM mostly uses three areas for our code:
- Stack: small, fast memory. Every method call gets its own box here (called a frame) holding its local variables.
- Heap: the big shared area where objects live.
- Metaspace (class area): where the JVM keeps information about each class, like its name, its methods and their bytecode.
Here is how they look together:
        STACK                       HEAP                    METASPACE
   (method call boxes)         (objects live here)        (class info)
  +------------------+      +----------------------+   +------------------+
  | main()           |      |  Dog object          |   | Dog class        |
  |  d1 -----------------+->|   name = "Bruno"     |   |  - bark()        |
  +------------------+   |  |   age  = 3           |   |  - constructor   |
                         |  +----------------------+   +------------------+
                         |
                         +-- d1 only holds the ADDRESS of the object
Keep this picture in your head. One idea matters most: a variable like d1 does not hold the object. It holds a reference, which you can think of as the object's address.
Classes and objects
A class is a blueprint. An object is a real thing built from that blueprint.
public class Dog {
    String name;
    int age;
    Dog(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
    void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " says Woof!");
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d1 = new Dog("Bruno", 3);
        d1.bark();
    }
}
Output:
Bruno says Woof!


  What happens when you write new Dog("Bruno", 3)
This one line does quite a lot. In simple steps:
- 
Load the class. If the JVM hasn't seen Dog yet, it loads Dog.class and stores the class info in Metaspace.
- 
Allocate memory. The JVM reserves space on the heap for one Dog object. That space holds name, age, plus a small hidden header (it includes a link back to the class).
- 
Set default values. Before your code runs, every field gets a default: 0 for numbers, false for booleans, null for references.
- Run the constructor. Your constructor fills in the real values.
- 
Return the reference. The address of the new object is stored in d1 on the stack.
You can see this in the bytecode. If you run javap -c Dog (a tool that ships with the JDK), you'll see something close to this inside main:
new           Dog          // create the object on the heap
dup                        // keep a copy of the reference
ldc           "Bruno"
iconst_3
invokespecial Dog.<init>   // run the constructor
astore_1                   // store the reference in d1
The exact lines can vary a little by Java version, but the idea is always the same: create, then construct, then store.
Why this matters: references are copied, not objects
Dog d1 = new Dog("Bruno", 3);
Dog d2 = d1;          // copies the address, NOT the object
d2.name = "Tiger";
System.out.println(d1.name);
Output:
Tiger
Surprised? Both d1 and d2 point to the same object on the heap. There is only one Dog.
  d1 ----\
          +--->  [ Dog: name="Tiger", age=3 ]
  d2 ----/
Once this clicked for me, a lot of "weird bugs" stopped being weird.


  What is this?
Inside a constructor or method, this means "the object I'm working on right now."
Behind the scenes, every instance method secretly receives the object as a hidden first argument. You can think of d1.bark() as bark(d1). That's how one bark() method, stored once in Metaspace, works correctly for every Dog. It knows which dog to use because the object is passed in.
Static vs instance
- 
Instance fields and methods belong to each object. Every Dog has its own name.
- Static fields and methods belong to the class. There is only one copy, shared by all objects.
class Counter {
    static int total = 0;   // one shared copy
    int id;                 // one per object
    Counter() {
        total++;
        id = total;
    }
}
If you create three Counter objects, Counter.total is 3, and each object has its own id.
Encapsulation: protect your data
Encapsulation means keeping your fields private and letting others touch them only through methods you control.
class BankAccount {
    private double balance;
    void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Invalid amount");
            return;
        }
        balance += amount;
    }
    double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
BankAccount acc = new BankAccount();
acc.deposit(500);
acc.deposit(-100);
System.out.println(acc.getBalance());
Output:
Invalid amount
500.0
Why bother? Because if balance were public, anyone could write acc.balance = -99999. With a private field, all changes go through deposit, and that's where you put your rules. If the rules change later, you fix one place.
Behind the scenes, the compiler refuses to compile code that touches a private field from outside the class, and the JVM checks access again when the code runs. (Advanced tools like reflection can bypass this, but normal code can't.)
Inheritance: reuse and extend
Inheritance lets one class build on another using extends. The child gets the parent's fields and methods.
class Animal {
    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal constructor");
    }
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating...");
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
    void bark() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}
Dog d = new Dog();
d.eat();
d.bark();
Output:
Animal constructor
Dog constructor
Eating...
Woof!
What happens behind the scenes
When you create a Dog, the heap object contains the parent's fields and the child's fields together, in one object. There's no separate Animal object hiding inside.
  Dog object on the heap
  +---------------------------+
  | header                    |
  | Animal fields (inherited) |
  | Dog fields (its own)      |
  +---------------------------+
Also notice the output order: "Animal constructor" printed first. The first line of every constructor secretly calls the parent's constructor with super(), unless you write it yourself. So the chain always runs from the top parent down to the child. At the very top of every chain sits the class Object, which every Java class extends.
Polymorphism: one call, many behaviors
Polymorphism means "many forms." In Java it shows up in two ways, and the difference is all about when Java decides which method to run.
1. Method overloading (decided at compile time)
Same method name, different parameters.
class Printer {
    void print(int x)    { System.out.println("int: " + x); }
    void print(String s) { System.out.println("String: " + s); }
}
Printer p = new Printer();
p.print(10);
p.print("Hi");
Output:
int: 10
String: Hi
The compiler looks at the argument types and picks the right version before the program even runs.
2. Method overriding (decided at run time)
A child class replaces the parent's version of a method.
class Animal {
    void sound() { System.out.println("Some sound"); }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() { System.out.println("Woof"); }
}
class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    void sound() { System.out.println("Meow"); }
}
Animal a1 = new Dog();
Animal a2 = new Cat();
a1.sound();
a2.sound();
Output:
Woof
Meow
Look closely. Both variables have the type Animal, yet different methods run. How does Java know?
The behind-the-scenes magic: dynamic dispatch
The compiler can't know whether a1 will hold a Dog or a Cat, because that could change while the program runs. So it doesn't decide. Instead, it writes a bytecode instruction (invokevirtual) that means "look at the real object and find its method."
At run time, the JVM follows the object's header to its class, then looks in that class's method table for sound(). A Dog object leads to Dog's table, so you get "Woof".
  a1 (type Animal) ---> [ Dog object ] ---> Dog class ---> sound() => "Woof"
  a2 (type Animal) ---> [ Cat object ] ---> Cat class ---> sound() => "Meow"
Real JVMs use clever tricks to make this lookup very fast, but the idea is just that: the method is chosen by the real object, not by the variable's type. This is called dynamic dispatch, and it's the engine behind polymorphism.
The @Override annotation is worth using every time. If you misspell the method name or get a parameter wrong, the compiler gives you an error instead of silently creating a new method.
Abstraction: show what, hide how
Abstraction means exposing what something does and hiding how it does it. Java gives you two tools: abstract classes and interfaces.
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();   // no body: children must provide it
    void describe() {
        System.out.println("Area is " + area());
    }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    double r;
    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
    double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}
class Rectangle extends Shape {
    double w, h;
    Rectangle(double w, double h) { this.w = w; this.h = h; }
    double area() { return w * h; }
}
Shape s1 = new Circle(2);
Shape s2 = new Rectangle(3, 4);
s1.describe();
s2.describe();
Output:
Area is 12.566370614359172
Area is 12.0
You can't write new Shape(), because an abstract class is incomplete on purpose. The compiler stops you. But notice how nicely it works with polymorphism: describe() is written once, and it calls area(), which resolves to the right version through dynamic dispatch.
An interface is a pure contract. It says "any class that signs this must have these methods."
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}
class Bird implements Flyable {
    public void fly() { System.out.println("Bird flaps its wings"); }
}
class Plane implements Flyable {
    public void fly() { System.out.println("Plane uses its engines"); }
}
A bird and a plane have nothing in common in real life, yet both can be used as Flyable. A class can extend only one parent class, but it can implement many interfaces, which is one reason interfaces are so popular.
What happens when objects are no longer needed?
You never delete objects in Java. The garbage collector does it for you.
Dog d = new Dog("Bruno", 3);
d = null;   // nothing points to the Dog now
After this, no reference points to the Dog object on the heap. The JVM sees that nobody can reach it anymore, and it may free that memory later. You don't control exactly when, and that's by design. It saves you from a whole class of memory bugs common in languages like C.
Which one should I use?
Here is how I decide between the common choices:
- Inheritance or composition? Use inheritance only for a true "is-a" relationship (a Dog is an Animal). If it's a "has-a" relationship (a Car has an Engine), put an Engine object inside Car instead. Composition is more flexible and doesn't lock you into a deep family tree.
- Abstract class or interface? Use an abstract class when related classes share code or fields (like Shape). Use an interface when you only need a contract, especially for unrelated classes (like Flyable).
- Overloading or overriding? Overload when the same action needs different inputs. Override when a child needs its own version of the parent's behavior.
- 
Static or instance? If the data or method belongs to each object, make it instance. If it's shared by all, or it's a pure helper that needs no object (like Math.max), make it static.
Common mistakes
- 
Thinking d2 = d1 copies the object. It only copies the reference. Both variables now point to one object. If you need a real copy, create a new object.
- 
Comparing objects with ==. For objects, == checks whether both sides are the same object in memory, not whether they look equal. Use .equals(), and override equals() and hashCode() in your own classes when equality should depend on values.
- Making fields public. It's quick, but it gives up control. Use private fields with methods.
- 
Expecting fields to be polymorphic. Only instance methods are chosen by the real object. Fields and static methods are chosen by the variable's type. So Animal a = new Dog(); reading a.name uses Animal's field, even if Dog has its own name.
- Overusing inheritance. Deep family trees become hard to change. Prefer composition when "is-a" doesn't clearly fit.
- 
Forgetting to create the object. Dog d; or Dog d = null; followed by d.bark() throws a NullPointerException, because there is no object at that address.
Quick recap
- A class is a blueprint, an object is a real thing on the heap, and a variable holds only a reference to it.
- new loads the class, allocates heap memory, sets default values, runs the constructor and returns a reference.
- Encapsulation: private fields, public methods, one place for your rules.
- Inheritance: one object holds parent and child fields, and constructors run from parent to child.
- Polymorphism: overloading is decided by the compiler, overriding is decided at run time by the real object (dynamic dispatch).
- Abstraction: abstract classes and interfaces define what, children define how.
- The garbage collector frees objects that nobody points to anymore.
Try it yourself
Here's a small practice idea: build a tiny zoo. Make an abstract class Animal with a sound() method, add three child classes, put them all in an Animal[] array, and loop through it calling sound(). Then add a static int count that goes up each time an animal is created. You'll use almost everything from this post in about 30 lines.
If you want to go one step deeper, run javap -c on your own class and read the bytecode. Seeing new, invokespecial and invokevirtual with your own eyes makes all of this feel real.
If this helped, or if you spotted something I should fix, tell me in the comments. I'm still learning too.

Java Decisions and Loops: if, switch, while, do-while, for and the Enhanced for
Up to now, our Java programs ran from top to bottom, one line after another. Real programs aren't like that. They need to make choices ("is the user logged in?") and repeat things ("check every item in the cart").
Java gives us two groups of tools for this:
- 
Conditionals help the program decide: if, else if, else, and switch.
- 
Loops help the program repeat: while, do-while, for, and the enhanced for.
Let's go through each one, and I'll also share when to pick which, because that's the part tutorials often skip.
Part 1: Making decisions
if, else if, else
The if statement runs a block of code only when a condition is true.
int marks = 72;
if (marks >= 90) {
    System.out.println("Grade A");
} else if (marks >= 60) {
    System.out.println("Grade B");
} else {
    System.out.println("Grade C");
}
Java checks the conditions from top to bottom and runs the first one that is true. Then it skips the rest. Here, 72 is not >= 90, but it is >= 60, so we get "Grade B".
Two things to remember:
- The condition must be a boolean (true or false). Unlike some other languages, Java won't treat 1 or 0 as true or false.
- Order matters. If you checked marks >= 60 first, a student with 95 would also get "Grade B", because 95 is also >= 60. Put the strictest condition first.
A mistake almost everyone makes: comparing strings with ==.
String city = "Pune";
if (city.equals("Pune")) {   // correct
    System.out.println("Welcome!");
}
For objects like String, == checks whether both sides are the same object in memory, not whether the text is the same. Use .equals() to compare the text.
The ternary operator (a shortcut)
For a simple either/or choice, you can write an if-else on one line:
int age = 20;
String result = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
Read it as: "if age >= 18, use 'Adult', otherwise use 'Minor'." It's handy for small things. If you find yourself nesting them, go back to a normal if, because they get hard to read very fast.
switch
When you compare one value against many fixed options, a long else if ladder gets messy. That's where switch is cleaner.
The classic style looks like this:
int day = 3;
String name;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        name = "Monday";
        break;
    case 2:
        name = "Tuesday";
        break;
    case 3:
        name = "Wednesday";
        break;
    default:
        name = "Unknown";
}
See the break? It's important. Without it, Java doesn't stop at the matching case. It keeps running into the next case too. This is called fall-through:
int day = 1;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
}
// Prints both "Monday" and "Tuesday"
Sometimes fall-through is useful (several cases sharing one result), but most of the time it's a bug waiting to happen.
The modern way (Java 14 and later) fixes this with arrows. No break, no fall-through, and the switch can even return a value:
int day = 6;
String type = switch (day) {
    case 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 -> "Weekday";
    case 6, 7          -> "Weekend";
    default            -> "Invalid day";
};
This is shorter and safer. If your project uses a recent Java version, prefer this style.
if or switch? Use if when your conditions are ranges or complex (marks >= 60 && marks < 90). Use switch when you're matching one value against a list of exact options, like days, menu choices or status codes.
Part 2: Repeating things with loops
Every loop has the same three ingredients:
- A starting point
- A condition that decides whether to keep going
- An update that moves you toward the end
If you forget the third one, your loop never stops. That's an infinite loop, and it's the most common loop bug.
while
Use while when you don't know in advance how many times you'll repeat. The condition is checked before each round.
int count = 3;
while (count > 0) {
    System.out.println("Countdown: " + count);
    count--;
}
System.out.println("Liftoff!");
Output:
Countdown: 3
Countdown: 2
Countdown: 1
Liftoff!
Notice count--. That's our update. Remove it, and count stays 3 forever.
Since the check happens first, a while loop might run zero times. If count started at 0, nothing would print.
do-while
A do-while loop is almost the same, but the condition is checked after the body. That means it always runs at least once.
int number;
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
do {
    System.out.print("Enter a number between 1 and 10: ");
    number = sc.nextInt();
} while (number < 1 || number > 10);
This is a perfect fit for "ask the user first, then validate." You can't check their answer before you've asked the question. Notice the semicolon after the while (...). It's required here, and people forget it.
while or do-while? If the loop body must run at least one time (menus, input checks), use do-while. Otherwise, while is the usual choice.
for
When you know how many times to repeat, for is the neatest option because it puts all three ingredients on one line:
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    System.out.println("Round " + i);
}
Read the header in three parts:
- int i = 1 is the start. It runs once.
- i <= 5 is the condition. It's checked before every round.
- i++ is the update. It runs after every round.
A very common mistake is the off-by-one error. Should it be i < 5 or i <= 5? Zero-based counting (like arrays) usually uses <. Counting from 1 usually uses <=. When in doubt, test with a small number and check by hand.
A for loop is really just a while loop with tidier packaging. Here's the same thing as a while:
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println("Round " + i);
    i++;
}
Both work. The for version keeps the counter's setup and update in one place, so you're less likely to forget the update.
Enhanced for (for-each)
When you just want to go through every item in an array or a list, the enhanced for loop removes the extra noise:
String[] fruits = {"Apple", "Banana", "Mango"};
for (String fruit : fruits) {
    System.out.println(fruit);
}
Read the : as "in". So: "for each fruit in fruits". There's no counter, no length check, and no chance of going past the end of the array.
It works on lists too:
List<Integer> scores = List.of(80, 92, 75);
int total = 0;
for (int score : scores) {
    total += score;
}
But it has limits. Use a normal for instead when:
- You need the index (position) of each item.
- You want to go backwards or skip items (like every second one).
- You want to change the array's elements by position. The loop variable is just a copy of each value, so fruit = "Kiwi" won't change the array.
- You're removing items from a list while looping. That throws a ConcurrentModificationException.
break and continue
Two small keywords give you extra control inside any loop:
- break stops the loop completely.
- continue skips the rest of the current round and moves to the next one.
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;   // skip 3
    if (i == 6) break;      // stop at 6
    System.out.println(i);
}
// Prints: 1, 2, 4, 5
Use them when they make the code clearer, but don't overdo it. A loop full of break and continue is usually a sign that the logic can be written more simply.
So which one should I use?
Here's the quick way I think about it:
- 
Choosing between two or three paths? if / else if / else
- 
Matching one value against many exact options? switch (the arrow style if you can)
- 
Repeat while something is true, and you don't know how many times? while
- 
Must run at least once? do-while
- 
You know how many rounds, or you need the index? for
- 
Just visit every item in an array or list? enhanced for
Common mistakes to watch for
- Forgetting the update (i++) and creating an infinite loop.
- Off-by-one errors with < and <=.
- Comparing strings with == instead of .equals().
- Forgetting break in an old-style switch and getting fall-through.
- Putting a semicolon right after if (...) or for (...), which makes the body run separately from the condition.
Wrapping up
Everything here comes down to two ideas: decide and repeat. Once you're comfortable with them, you can build almost any logic out of these small pieces, and the choice between them is mostly about picking the one that makes your intention clearest to the next person who reads your code (often you, a month later).
A good way to practice: write a small number-guessing game. It uses an if to compare guesses, a do-while to keep asking, and maybe a switch for a hint menu. Try it and see how the pieces fit together.
If this helped, or if you spotted something I should fix, let me know in the comments. I'm still learning too.

Munchable has no ulid dependency. It has this, in apps/web/lib/ulid.ts:
// Dependency-free ULID generator (lean mandate: no `ulid` package for ~20
// lines of code). 48-bit millisecond timestamp + 80 bits of crypto randomness,
// Crockford base32, lexically sortable by creation time. Used as the app-side
// primary key for catalog.product_revisions (the schema deliberately has no DB
// default for it).
const ENC = '0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ';
export function ulid(now: number = Date.now()): string {
  let ts = '';
  let t = now;
  for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    ts = ENC[t % 32] + ts;
    t = Math.floor(t / 32);
  }
  const bytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));
  let rand = '';
  for (let i = 0; i < 16; i++) rand += ENC[bytes[i] % 32];
  return ts + rand;
}
Five call sites use it: a contribution revision, a settled reward row, a support ticket, a support message, and an entry in the curated ingredient overlay. What I want to write about is not the twenty lines. It is the three separate things we were originally asking a single identifier to be, and the bug that lives in the gap between them.
Why the generator is app side and the column has no default
The schema comment is blunt about it:
// ULID, generated app side (lexically sortable by time). No DB default.
id: text('id').primaryKey(),
A gen_random_uuid() default is the obvious alternative and it was the wrong shape for us, because the insert order is load bearing. A capture writes an append-only revision row first, and then upserts the product row with current_revision_id pointing at it. If the database invents the id, the application does not know it until the insert comes back, and the write becomes two round trips with a window in between. Generating it first makes both writes part of one statement sequence the caller fully describes before it starts.
Leaving the column without a default is the deliberate half of that. An insert that forgets the id fails loudly at the boundary instead of silently minting a value whose format nothing else in the codebase expects.
The two characters nobody counts
Ten base32 characters hold 50 bits. A millisecond timestamp needs 48. That slack is why the loop can be a plain t % 32 with no masking: the value cannot overflow ten characters until long after the heat death of this product.
It also means every id sorts correctly as text, which is the actual reason for the format. Crockford base32 drops I, L, O and U to avoid transcription errors, and the remaining alphabet is still in ascending ASCII order:
0123456789ABCDEFGHJKMNPQRSTVWXYZ
So a lexicographic comparison of two ids is a numeric comparison of their timestamps. Postgres sorts them with a plain B-tree on text, no expression index, no custom collation, and a keyset-paginated admin queue gets a tiebreak that happens to be creation order.
One honest limitation: there is no monotonic counter inside a millisecond. Two ids minted in the same millisecond are ordered by their random tails, which is to say arbitrarily. Everything we use the ordering for needs a stable total order rather than true creation order at sub-millisecond resolution, so this is fine, but it is the kind of thing that is worth knowing before you build a feature on top of it.
128 bits drawn, 80 bits kept, zero bias
The random half looks wasteful, and it is:
const bytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));
for (let i = 0; i < 16; i++) rand += ENC[bytes[i] % 32];
Sixteen bytes is 128 bits of randomness, and the result keeps 80 of them: five bits per character, sixteen characters. The other 48 bits are thrown away. Drawing one byte per output character and discarding three of its bits is a line of code anybody can read, and the entropy saved by bit-packing has no value to spend.
What matters is that % 32 is exactly the case where modulo is not biased. 256 divides by 32 with no remainder, so each of the 32 characters has precisely eight of the 256 byte values mapping to it. Uniform in, uniform out.
Change the alphabet length and that evaporates. Base62 is the usual example:
256 = 4 × 62 + 8
Residues 0 through 7 get five byte values each, residues 8 through 61 get four. The first eight characters of your alphabet come out roughly 25 per cent more often than the rest. That is the bug in every hand-rolled short-id helper that reaches for bytes[i] % alphabet.length, and it is invisible in testing because the ids still look random. It is only safe here because of a property of the number 32, not because modulo is fine.
The three jobs, separated
A support ticket is where the pressure on a single identifier showed up, because the id was being asked to be three incompatible things at once.
One: the primary key. The ULID. Sortable, opaque, appears in URLs the signed-in owner can see.
Two: something a human can read back to you over email. A 26-character string is not that, so there is a reference derived from it:
export function ticketRef(id: string): string {
  return `MU-${id.slice(-6).toUpperCase()}`;
}
Six characters off the random tail, not the timestamp head. Taking it off the front would give near-identical refs to two tickets opened in the same second, which is precisely when a support queue most needs to tell two things apart. The ref is 32 to the power of 6, or 1,073,741,824 possibilities, and by the birthday bound two tickets would share one at around 38,000 tickets. That is acceptable only because the ref is a label, never a lookup key: it is printed in the acknowledgement email and in the admin list, and no code path resolves a ticket from it. If it had become a lookup key, that number would be a liability.
Three: the secret that authorises an inbound email onto the thread. Not the id. The id loses that competition the moment it is allowed into a URL. So it is a separate value from a separate generator:
const id = ulid();
const threadToken = crypto.randomUUID().replace(/-/g, '');
Support replies come back by email, and the thread token lives in the Reply-To address. That address is the only thing proving an inbound message belongs on a particular conversation, which makes it a bearer credential. It never appears in a DTO, never reaches a client, and goes to the route only so the route can build the Reply-To header. If the sortable id in the URL had doubled as that token, anyone holding a ticket link could append to someone else's thread.
Sortable, memorable, unguessable. Pick any one per value.
See it working
- 
munchable.app/support is the form. Submit anything and the confirmation hands you back an MU- reference, which is the six characters above. You do not need an account to use it, which is the point: the person who cannot sign in is the one who most needs to reach us.
- munchable.app/licenses explains what an accepted capture becomes, which is the append-only revision row whose primary key this generator exists for.
- munchable.app/answers is the published side of the curated ingredient data, where the overlay entries that use the fifth call site end up, as pages like is sorbitol low FODMAP.
If you have a hand-rolled id helper in your codebase, the one thing worth checking today is the alphabet length. Thirty-two and sixty-four are fine. Anything that does not divide 256 is quietly skewing your ids, and nothing will ever tell you.

