When you compare email and SMS APIs for gaming support, the event notification infrastructure is useful only if it reaches the queue that can act before the player gives up. Delivery reliability, not channel price, changes the design: persist the event first, route it deterministically, and treat both channels as replaceable delivery adapters rather than as the system of record.
TL;DR: Use email for the normal support workflow, reserve SMS for explicitly urgent and consented escalation, and put both behind a durable outbox with idempotent workers. A customer-engagement platform can own cross-channel policy when non-engineers need journeys and audience controls, but it does not remove the need for acceptance, delivery, and response SLOs.
Consider a bounded incident: a player selects account_takeover, the application sends an email inline, and the request returns success after the provider accepts it. The message is later suppressed, however, and no ticket exists because the form submission was never durably recorded. Retrying the form risks two notifications; doing nothing loses the case. The invariant is blunt: provider acceptance is not support-queue acceptance.
What failure can email and SMS APIs hide in event notification infrastructure?
Start with the failure boundary. An email or SMS API response normally establishes that one service accepted a request. It cannot establish that a mailbox accepted an email, that a handset received an SMS, or that an agent started work. SMTP distinguishes message transfer from later delivery outcomes, while HTTP defines status semantics and Retry-After; neither protocol turns a 2xx into an end-to-end support guarantee. Comparing API ergonomics before deciding which transitions the system must prove is backwards: a convenient request method does not repair a missing support case, and an elaborate engagement suite cannot define the platform team's error budget for it.
Acceptance isn't delivery.
Model the path as separate observable transitions: received -> classified -> queued -> provider_accepted -> delivered, followed by acknowledged when the support system takes ownership. Keep timestamps and a stable event ID at every boundary. A delayed delivery receipt must update state, never create a second case.
The incident lesson is that the queue boundary belongs close to the contact form. If routing or a downstream provider is unavailable, the form handler should commit the event and return only after that durable write succeeds. It should not wait for an external notification round trip. This advice does not apply to a fire-and-forget analytics event whose loss budget explicitly permits dropping data; a player reporting account compromise is not such an event.
Implement the queue boundary before selecting a channel
Start with a small policy surface. Inputs that affect routing must be explicit and validated: issue category, player region, consent, and urgency. Free-form text should be ticket content, not a hidden routing language. Region selects an operational queue and data-handling path; it should not be guessed from a phone prefix.
The following Go program uses an in-memory outbox so it runs as written. In production, replace the map with a transactional database table written in the same commit as the support event. The unique event ID is the idempotency boundary.
package main
import (
    "context"
    "errors"
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)
type ContactEvent struct {
    ID, Category, Region, Email, Phone string
    Urgent, SMSOptIn                   bool
}
type Job struct { EventID, Queue, Channel, State string }
type Outbox struct {
    mu sync.Mutex
    jobs map[string]Job
}
func route(e ContactEvent) (string, string, error) {
    if e.ID == "" || e.Email == "" {
        return "", "", errors.New("event ID and email are required")
    }
    queue := "game-support-us"
    if e.Region == "eu" { queue = "game-support-eu" }
    if e.Category == "account_takeover" { queue += "-security" }
    channel := "email"
    if e.Urgent && e.SMSOptIn && e.Phone != "" { channel = "sms" }
    return queue, channel, nil
}
func (o *Outbox) Accept(_ context.Context, e ContactEvent) (Job, error) {
    queue, channel, err := route(e)
    if err != nil { return Job{}, err }
    o.mu.Lock()
    defer o.mu.Unlock()
    if existing, ok := o.jobs[e.ID]; ok { return existing, nil }
    job := Job{e.ID, queue, channel, "queued"}
    o.jobs[e.ID] = job
    return job, nil
}
func main() {
    store := &Outbox{jobs: make(map[string]Job)}
    event := ContactEvent{
        ID: "contact-8f3a", Category: "account_takeover", Region: "eu",
        Urgent: true, SMSOptIn: true, Email: "player@example.test",
        Phone: "+1555010100",
    }
    job, err := store.Accept(context.Background(), event)
    if err != nil { panic(err) }
    fmt.Printf("%s %s %s\n", job.EventID, job.Queue, job.Channel)
}
This sample deliberately does not send a notification. Persistence and dispatch are different failure domains, and combining them in one handler makes timeout behavior ambiguous. A worker should claim a queued row, invoke a channel adapter with a deadline, record the provider's opaque message ID, and retry only errors classified as transient. Use capped exponential backoff with jitter; honor Retry-After when an HTTP service supplies it. Permanent validation failures belong in a review queue, not an infinite retry loop.
Timeouts lie.
Budget for backlog recovery before channel selection
Email, SMS, and engagement suites solve overlapping but different parts of the path. Email carries context well and fits an ordinary queue workflow. SMS is constrained, interruptive, and tied to consent and phone-number quality, so it earns its place as an escalation channel rather than a universal fallback. A cross-channel engagement suite adds audience state, campaign or journey authoring, and governance surfaces; that can reduce application-side policy code while increasing dependency breadth and migration cost.
Do the capacity calculation before choosing an interface. If the peak contact rate is 40 events per second and each event can create one primary notification plus one escalation, provision for 80 dispatches per second before retry amplification. At a 1% transient retry rate, average added load is small, but a provider outage can release a large backlog at once. Cap worker concurrency, drain at a rate the downstream accepts, and measure the age of the oldest queued job. That age is usually a better paging signal than raw queue depth.
Choose SLOs per transition. One defensible starting point is 99.9% of valid forms durably queued within 2 seconds and 99% of urgent jobs accepted by a configured channel within 60 seconds; these are example engineering targets, not industry benchmarks. Delivery and agent acknowledgement need separate objectives because they have different owners. Burn-rate alerts should page on the user-facing objective, while provider error counts remain diagnostic signals.
| Approach | Policy ownership | Operational burden | Lock-in surface | Best fit | 
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Direct email and SMS APIs | Application team | Two adapters, receipts, suppression handling, and queue operations | Provider payloads and delivery semantics | Stable rules with strong platform ownership | 
| Cross-channel engagement suite | Operations team with engineering guardrails | Identity sync, journey governance, webhooks, and suite availability | Audience model, templates, and journey definitions | Frequently changing multi-step policy | 
| Self-hosted dispatch layer | Platform team | Storage, workers, security, upgrades, and on-call coverage | Internal schema and tooling | Control needs that justify permanent ownership | 
This is a buy-versus-build decision, not a feature-count contest. Resend documents an email API, while Customer.io, Braze, and OneSignal document broader messaging or engagement platforms. Those categories imply different boundaries: a direct email interface still leaves channel orchestration in your backend; a broader platform moves more orchestration into its data model and control plane. Verify current regional processing, retention, consent, export, and rate-limit terms during procurement because those details can change and the public interfaces do not settle your compliance obligations.
Each option has a hard limitation. Direct APIs are not suitable when a non-engineering team must frequently change multi-step audience journeys without deployments. A broad engagement suite is a poor fit when the only job is deterministic contact-form routing and the team cannot justify identity synchronization, another control plane, and a wider incident surface. Self-hosting has the clearest control boundary, but its trade-off is permanent upgrade, security, capacity, and on-call ownership; do not choose it when those duties lack an explicit team and error budget.
Inject duplicate and out-of-order state transitions
A happy-path request proves almost nothing. Test a timeout after the downstream accepted the request, duplicated delivery receipts, receipts arriving out of order, a malformed address, revoked SMS consent, a region with no route, and a backlog replay after an hour-long outage. The acceptance criterion is not merely “no error”: one contact event must create one support case, state must move monotonically, and every terminal failure must be queryable by event ID.
Run adapter contract tests against a fake HTTP server and keep provider-specific fixtures at the boundary. Then inject failures in staging with the same retry ceilings used in production. A deploy is safe only when old workers can read new jobs and new workers can read old jobs; expand the schema first, deploy readers second, and remove obsolete fields later.
Observability needs three kinds of correlation: the contact event ID, the support case ID, and the provider message ID. Do not put the player's message, email address, or phone number in metric labels. Counters can track outcomes by low-cardinality channel, queue, and error class; traces can carry opaque IDs; restricted logs can hold the minimum detail needed for investigation. Retention should follow the support and privacy policy rather than the convenience of debugging.
No silent drops.
Preserve an operational exit before committing
Run the same workload through candidate adapters and score the operational boundary, not a polished dashboard. Measure queue-to-accept latency, receipt completeness, duplicate behavior, recovery after throttling, backlog drain time, and the work required to answer “what happened to event contact-8f3a?” Include on-call ownership and migration effort beside recurring cost. Price belongs in the model, but delivery reliability is the gate.
Keep a narrow internal contract: send a typed notification request, receive an internal attempt ID, and ingest normalized status events. Templates may remain channel-specific, but routing rules and consent decisions should live in a portable policy layer unless the team deliberately accepts platform ownership. Export configuration and test fixtures periodically. An adapter that cannot be replaced without rewriting the contact form is too wide.
The resulting design is deliberately unexciting: durable intake, deterministic routing, bounded retries, explicit consent, and independent SLOs. That is what makes a gaming support notification dependable. Channel selection can change later without risking the report that started the workflow.

Just looking at what Bramus shared the other day:
/* Adding a base class */
.btn {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
/* Listing everything... yuck! */
.btn-primary,
.btn-secondary,
.btn-danger {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
/* Works, but performs badly */
[class^="btn-"],
[class*=" btn-"] {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
}
/* Newly resolved class prefix selector */
.btn-* {
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border-radius: 4px;
}
First off, if you don’t follow Bramus, where have you been?! Jokes aside, his job is to be first-in-line on new features like this — especially as it pertains to Chrome — so it’s worth keeping tabs on his RSS and/or social.
It’s not a new proposal. Lea posted it back in 2024 and has advocated for it the whole while. As Bramus mentions in his post, what’s new is that the proposal was formally adopted and, as of three days ago, it has been added to the Selectors Level 5 spec draft. So, chances are that we’ll see it formally adopted at some point and implemented somewhere even sooner.
I really like the ergonomics of it. Existing substring selectors — class^="prefix" and class=*" prefix" — are verbose and defintely less readable than a simple .prefix-*.
And it’s not like [data-attribute] selectors that require not only an extra step touching HTML but still added verbosity.
But something makes me wince at the idea. I can’t quite put my finger on it. Perhaps it’s redundancy as in, we can already do this with existing selectors? Bramus cites performance issues with existing substring selectors as a primary reason we need this. But Brian Kardell’s reply resonates with me:
Then again, I do like how we extended color functions for brevity, like:
/* old */
color: hsla(100, 50%, 50%, .5);
/* new */
color: hsl(100 50 50% / .5);
And it’s backwards-compatible, so no real harm if you continue to use the “old” way. It’s not like substring selectors don’t have other use cases and become totally obsolete. But maybe that’s it: this isn’t an “upgrade” of something we already have, but a new thing that isn’t progressive enhancement out of the gate. We’d have to @support it until it becomes a Baseline feature:
@supports selector(.prefix-*) {
  /* ... */
}
…which may or may not be a long wait. But we don’t know. And if ergonomics are the selling point, then we’re losing that in the wait.
Should also note that the wildcard doesn’t match other conditions or non-dashed cases:
/* Nope */
.prefix* {}
.prefix-*-suffix {}
.prefix_* {} /* the door is left open on this */
Another worthy note is that the spec currently implies (but doesn’t explicitly state) that this has the same specificity as a class selector, (0,1,0). That’d make sense, as .prefix-* is really no different than writing .prefix-variation.
That said, I like how it might possibly look in a nested syntax:
.prefix {
  /* This would work, right? */
  &-* { /* ... */ }
}
…and Dave’s plea to support selecting web components:
Maybe I just convinced myself that I like it. Again, I dunno. Just take the added convenience and move on! Yada yada.

